---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ResultPanel from '../components/ResultPanel.astro';
import { YIELDS } from '../lib/effects';
import { DEFAULT_CITY, cities, getCity } from '../lib/cities';
import type { YieldKt } from '../lib/effects';

const city = getCity(DEFAULT_CITY) ?? cities[0];
const defaultYield: YieldKt = 100;
const defaultBurst = 'air' as const;

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How far from a 100 kt explosion does the blast do damage?',
    a: 'At 100 kt the 5 psi ring reaches 3.22 km and the 1 psi ring 9.93 km, both read from Fig. 3.73c of The Effects of Nuclear Weapons and scaled by the cube-root law printed in that manual (§3.61, p. 101). One psi is the level at which window glass breaks; five psi is the level associated with most residential buildings being destroyed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this a simulation?',
    a: 'No. Every number on this page is transcribed from a 1977 U.S. government manual and carries the figure and printed page it came from. Nothing is modelled, fitted or interpolated beyond the scaling law the manual itself states.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the same yield give different radii for air and contact bursts?',
    a: 'A contact burst puts the fireball against the ground, so part of the energy goes into the ground and the shock wave reflects differently. The manual plots both cases; this site reads each one from its own edge of the chart rather than adjusting one to look like the other.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are there no thermal radii for a contact burst?',
    a: 'Fig. 7.42, the chart the thermal values come from, is drawn for air bursts at a burst height of 200·W^0.4 ft. The manual handles surface-burst thermal radiation by a separate route (§7.41, p. 290). Rather than blend two procedures into one column, this page reports blast and fireball only for contact bursts.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="Nuke Simulator: Blast Radius for Any City, 15 kt to 1 Mt"
  description="Pick a city and a yield and see the fireball, 5 psi, 1 psi and thermal radii, drawn to scale. Every value is transcribed from The Effects of Nuclear Weapons (1977) with its figure and printed page."
  path="/"
  current="home"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Public-domain source · no model output</p>
  <h1>Pick a city, pick a yield — the rings are drawn to scale from the manual</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Five distances, three yields, two burst modes. Every number comes from
    <em>The Effects of Nuclear Weapons</em> (1977), and every number carries the figure and printed
    page it was read from — so you can check any of them against the book instead of taking this
    page's word for it.
  </p>

  <ResultPanel city={city} y={defaultYield} burst={defaultBurst} cities={cities} />

  <h2>What each ring means</h2>

  <p>
    The <strong>fireball</strong> is the incandescent sphere at the moment the shock front separates
    from it. <strong>5 psi</strong> is the overpressure associated with most residential buildings
    being destroyed; <strong>1 psi</strong> is roughly where window glass breaks. The two
    <strong>thermal</strong> rings are the slant range to a given irradiance:
    4.5&nbsp;cal/cm² for second-degree burns on unshaded light skin, 10&nbsp;cal/cm² for
    third-degree. Clothing, shelter, haze and skin tone all move the thermal figure, and this page
    makes no adjustment for any of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Height of burst is not chosen here. Each overpressure contour is read at its own peak — the
    point where it reaches furthest from ground zero — so every value carries the burst height the
    chart itself associates with that maximum. The panel shows that height next to each number, and
    the <a href="/blast-radius/">full table</a> carries it for all three yields.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/blast-radius/">Blast radius table →</a></h3>
      <p>
        All 24 values in one table: five effects, three yields, air and contact burst. Each cell
        gives the number, the burst height, the figure and the printed page, plus a tag saying
        whether it was read off a chart or scaled by the manual's own law. Readable with scripting
        off.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/methodology/">Methodology →</a></h3>
      <p>
        How each figure was read, the text of the scaling law, where the 1 Mt rows leave the chart's
        assumptions behind, and the one chart whose contour labels had to be identified from the
        manual's prose because the scan has no text layer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Questions this page answers</h2>

  <div class="faq">
    {
      faq.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>What this page is not.</strong> It gives distances from ground zero and nothing else —
    no casualties, no building damage at a specific address, no fallout. It does not cover
    underwater, underground or high-altitude bursts, multiple warheads, or terrain shielding. The
    <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> lists what is left out and why.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    Yields covered: {YIELDS.map((y) => `${y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt`).join(' · ')}.
    Ring centre for the default state: {city.name} ({city.lat.toFixed(4)}, {city.lon.toFixed(4)}) —
    a GeoNames gazetteer point, not an administrative centroid.
  </p>
</Base>
